2) a) https://arxiv.org/abs/0705.0993
Knutson and colleagues in the paper "A map of the day-night contrast of the extrasolar planet
HD 189733b" discussed the importance of observing star-planet systems that periodically eclipse to estimate dayside temperatures. In doing so, the key science question, "can the atmosphere transport energy incident to the planet's surface" can be investigated. Knutson et al. observed HD 189733, the closest eclipsing planetary system, and estimated dayside brightness to 973±33 K with a max at 1212±11 K at 8 µm. A hot spot shift was detected at  16±6
degrees before opposition and a secondary eclipse occurring at 120±24s. 
From the paper, I learned that HD 189733b is a hot Jupiter whose atmosphere efficiently transports heat from its dayside to its nightside. The hot spot directly faces the star, consistent with atmospheric winds transporting heat eastward. The 8-micron brightness temperatures range from 973 ± 33 K to 1212 ± 11 K, indicating a warm nightside. Its secondary eclipse occurred 120 ± 24 seconds later than predicted.

B) https://arxiv.org/abs/0802.0845
Using Spitzer Space Telescope series photometry, Charbonneau et al. estimated the relative eclipse depth of HD 189733 to 5 distinct bands with planet-to-star flux ratios to be 0.256 +/- 0.014% (3.6 microns), 0.214 +/- 0.020% (4.5 microns), 0.310 +/- 0.034% (5.8 microns), 0.391 +/- 0.022% (8.0 microns), and 0.598 +/- 0.038% (24 microns). They detected emission near 4 microns from an opacity window, disagreeing with the Plank spectrum and thus revealing atmospheric differences between HD 189733 and HD 209458b, a second researched exoplanet. Their data supports the idea that hot Jupiter atmospheres diverge into two classes with a thermal inversion layer for strong irradiated objects. 
From this paper I learned that the target HD 189733b’s dayside emission is best explained by an atmosphere without a temperature inversion. Its infrared emission deviates from a blackbody /Plank spectrum. Its emission near 4 microns is consistent with an opacity window. 


