C) https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.3239
Evans et al. investigated hot Jupiter HD189733b for a secondary eclipse observation using the Space Telescope Imaging Spectrograph on the Hubble Telescope. They measured the albedo at Ag = 0.40 \pm 0.12 across 290-450nm and Ag < 0.12 across 450-570nm at 1-sigma confidence. They then predicted albedos from three hot Jupiter models and found that the albedo decreasing toward longer wavelengths implies dayside optically thick reflective clouds. These results demonstrate the potential of secondary eclipse observations and more information is needed for more empirical constraints on HD 189733b’s atmosphere.

D)https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.22398
Using the Planetary Atmospheric Tool for Observer Noobs (PLATON), a computational program that contains NIRCam Grism Time Series observations, Zhang et al. presents 2.4–5.0 µm JWST/NIRCam emission spectrum of HD 189733b using their developed program to retrieve atmospheric parameters  for one eclipse in each of the F322W2 (2.4–3.95 µm) and F444W (3.90–5.00 µm)bandpasses. Their results prove that PLATON's advancements are suitable for the JWST observation era based on the retrieval of the necessary transmission and emission spectrums. However, they note factors such as the cloud parameterization, non-equilibrium chemistry, the definition of metallicity and C/O are may affect data accuracy.
While this paper was data analysis dense, I learned that HD 189733b has an atmosphere enriched in heavy elements like H₂O, CO₂, CO, and H₂S. It is depleted in methane at the terminator, the boundary between day and night probed during transit. 

